12. SIMPLE ENGLISH THROUGHOUT THE PLATFORM

This is extremely important.

UsedRight serves ordinary customers, not only technology experts.

Avoid unnecessarily technical terms.

Use simple, natural English that customers can understand immediately.

Do not make customers feel intimidated or discouraged from buying a device because of technical wording.

Replace complicated wording

Avoid	Use instead
Faulty	Not working
Face ID Failure	Face ID not working
SIM Locked	SIM locked to a network
Wi-Fi Only	Works with Wi-Fi only, no SIM
Battery Degradation	Battery does not last as long
Aftermarket Display	Replacement screen, not original
Factory OEM Display	Original manufacturer screen, if verified
Board Soldering / Rework	Main board has been repaired
Component Replacement	Part has been replaced
Cosmetic Defects	Signs of use or visible marks
Activation Lock	Device is linked to another owner’s account
Not Tested	Not tested yet

Do not use complicated technical expressions as the main customer-facing labels.

Where a technical term is necessary, provide a simple explanation beside it.

Product issue examples

Instead of making a customer feel that a device is unusable, present the information neutrally.

Example:

Face ID not working

You can still unlock and use the phone with its passcode. Face ID is not available.

Wi-Fi only

This device connects to the internet through Wi-Fi. It cannot use a SIM card for calls or mobile data.

Screen replaced

The screen has been changed. The vendor has provided information about the replacement.

Battery does not last as long

The battery condition is below the expected level. You may need to charge the device more often.

Important honesty rule

Simple language must not hide serious product issues.

All limitations must remain clearly visible.

Use a consistent red indicator for declared issues, but use calm, neutral wording.

Some customers may intentionally want a device with Wi-Fi-only functionality, a non-working Face ID or a replaced screen because it is more affordable.

Do not prevent purchases simply because a product has a disclosed issue.

Customers must be able to understand the issue and make an informed decision.

13. DELIVERY AND COLLECTION SYSTEM

Vendors should not have to guess delivery costs when creating a product listing.

The delivery cost depends on the customer’s location and delivery arrangements.

Remove mandatory vendor-entered delivery fees from the product listing form.

UsedRight delivery policy

For the initial Lagos launch, implement a configurable flat delivery fee of ₦5,000 where the delivery location is eligible.

UsedRight should control this fee.

Vendors should not individually enter the standard platform delivery charge.

The administrator should be able to:

* Set the standard Lagos delivery fee.
* Change the amount.
* Define eligible locations.
* Define excluded locations.
* Approve special delivery charges.
* Offer free delivery campaigns.
* Configure exceptional delivery arrangements.

Do not promise ₦5,000 delivery to locations that cannot reasonably be served at that price.

If a location requires a different arrangement, show the actual applicable charge before payment.

Customer delivery options

At checkout, allow customers to choose:

1. UsedRight-arranged delivery.
2. Physical pickup, if the vendor offers it.

Vendor pickup settings

Vendors can enable or disable physical pickup.

If enabled, they must provide:

* Pickup location.
* Pickup instructions.
* Available pickup hours.
* Contact information or approved communication method.

Do not display a vendor’s private residential address publicly without appropriate consent.

Customer pickup experience

If the customer selects physical pickup:

* Show the pickup location and instructions.
* Generate a unique collection reference or OTP.
* Require successful collection confirmation.
* Update the order status.

If the customer chooses delivery:

* Show the applicable delivery fee.
* Show the delivery method.
* Provide tracking or delivery status where available.

